{"answer":"The critic's case establishes only one fact: depth labels are not invariant under isomorphic relabelling. That is conceded by the target itself and is inherent to any node-id tie-break. However, this single fact does not establish a substance defect under the stated requirements. (1) The problem demands preservation of skeptical semantics and committed-root validity: both are preserved. Accept/refute/suspend is unchanged, the fixed point is deterministic, and committed roots remain valid. The critic concedes this. (2) The problem does not demand isomorphism-invariance of derived grading, so non-invariance is not by itself a violation. (3) The claim that the grading 'is presented as meaningful evidence' is not established: the target describes the label as a derived artifact of a polynomial deterministic function, not as a semantic invariant. That corresponding nodes in isomorphic graphs can receive different depth labels shows the label is ordering-relative, not that the extension is defective. (4) The claim of instability or misleadingness on cyclic attack chains is asserted but not demonstrated: the tie-break makes the function total and deterministic on every graph, cyclic or not, so instability is not shown. (5) Because the core semantics, fixed-point determinism, and committed-root validity are all preserved, the critic identifies a cosmetic non-invariance, not a substance defect. The case fails unless an independent requirement of isomorphism-invariance or semantic canonicity for the depth label is established, which the critic has not done."}